# ==== EQUIP HEADER · the separator between the generic-parameter list and the
#                     equipped type
#
# `equip [T] Cell[T]:` is the documented canonical spelling
# (`docs/language-reference.md`, every occurrence), and it is what the whole
# corpus is written in. `gg fmt` used to delete the space — the equip header
# emitter wrote the parameter list and then the type with nothing between them,
# alone among the language's headers.
#
# It RE-PARSES and it is IDEMPOTENT, which is exactly why no round-trip property
# could see it: `equip [T]Cell[T]:` is a well-formed header that means the same
# thing. What it is not is what the author wrote, and a bulk `gg fmt` sweep
# would have rewritten every generic `equip` header in the tree.
#
# POPULATION AND RULE, because "how many" was wrong three times: committed `.gg`
# SOURCE files only — a `.expected` golden copy is not swept, and this track's
# own equip fixtures are not pre-existing exposure:
#     grep -rn --include='*.gg' '^equip \[' lib tests/fixtures examples compiler \
#       | grep -v fmt_suite_layout/equip_generic_params_space.gg \
#       | grep -v fmt_suite_layout/header_colon_anchor_bracketed.gg
# → 41 headers across 9 files (regenerate; do not quote a remembered figure).
#
# AXIS — every shape the header's first render can take, because the separator
# is written once and each of these reaches it differently:
#   * plain            `equip [T] Cell[T]:`
#   * with-trait       `equip [T] Boxed[T] with Shown:`
#   * BOUNDED param    `equip [Shown T] Boxed[T] with Printed:`
#   * MULTI-param      `equip [K, V] Pair[K, V]:`
#   * via              `equip [T] Wrapper[T] via inner:`
#   * WRAPPED list     an over-width header whose bracket list BREAKS onto a
#     continuation line — the close `]` must STILL be followed by the separator
#     and the type. This is the cell the tail-reserve half of the fix serves:
#     the separator is part of what follows the list on its own last line, so
#     the list's width budget has to charge for it or the list is measured one
#     column narrower than it lands.
#
# ⚠ INPUT→OUTPUT, not a fixpoint — see the `.expected` companion: the wrapped
# cell's list is re-packed, so the authored spelling exists only on pass one.
#
# RED, measured against the pre-fix binary — this branch's tip immediately
# BEFORE the equip fix, not the round's base (the defect predates the whole
# track, so the round's base binary reds it for the same reason and proves
# nothing about this commit). Every generic cell came back welded:
#     equip [T]Cell[T]:                    equip [T]Boxed[T] with Shown:
#     equip [Shown T]Boxed[T] with Printed:   equip [K, V]Pair[K, V]:
#     equip [T]Wrapper[T] via inner:
#     …DDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDD,\n    EEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE]Wide[…   (wrapped)
# while the NON-GENERIC control `equip Plain:` was unaffected — which is what
# makes this the generic-params separator and not a general header defect.

trait Shown:
    String show(self)

trait Printed:
    String printed(self)

struct Cell[T]:
    T v

equip [T] Cell[T]:
    T get(self):
        return self.v

struct Boxed[T]:
    T inner

equip [T] Boxed[T] with Shown:
    String show(self):
        return "boxed"

equip [Shown T] Boxed[T] with Printed:
    String printed(self):
        return self.inner.show()

struct Pair[K, V]:
    K key
    V value

equip [K, V] Pair[K, V]:
    K first(self):
        return self.key

struct Wrapper[T]:
    T inner

equip [T] Wrapper[T] via inner:
    int tag(self):
        return 1

struct Wide[AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA, BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB, CCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCC, DDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDD,
    EEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE]:
    AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA a

equip [AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA, BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB, CCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCC, DDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDD,
    EEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE] Wide[AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA, BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB, CCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCC,
    DDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDDD, EEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE]:
    AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA first(self):
        return self.a

struct Plain:
    int v

equip Plain:
    int get(self):
        return self.v

void main():
    print(1)
